Gout
Rheumatology & Immunology
Acute podagra, Negatively birefringent crystals, Tophaceous deposits

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-019
Reason for Visit: Acute, excruciating pain, redness, and swelling involving the left great toe, completely preventing weight-bearing, sleep, or the ability to wear footwear.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a hyper-acute, intensely painful, hot, red monoarthritis of the first metatarsophalangeal joint. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out an acute skin/joint infection (cellulitis or septic arthritis) in the foot, which requires intravenous antibiotics, and to provide immediate, powerful anti-inflammatory therapy to restore mobility, as the pain was unmanageable with standard over-the-counter medications, justifying the visit prior to the next standard in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My big toe is on fire; it woke me up in absolute agony, and I can't even let the bedsheet touch it without screaming."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the sudden onset of intense, excruciating pain predominantly involving the left first metatarsophalangeal joint (acute podagra). The pain woke the patient from sleep approximately 8 hours ago and has peaked in intensity, accompanied by sudden intense erythema and swelling. The patient describes exquisite allodynia, where even light touch (such as a sock or bedsheet) is intolerable. Reports a low-grade fever and mild chills during the night. Patient has a history of similar, though milder, episodes in the past that completely resolved between attacks. Denies recent trauma to the toe. Notes drinking significant amounts of beer and eating a large steak dinner the night prior.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound/chairbound; completely unable to weight-bear on the left foot or ambulate.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to mobilize to access food or bathroom safely.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 95 Kg
General: Patient is sitting with the left foot elevated, visibly wincing and extremely guarded when the foot is approached.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal: The left first metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joint is severely swollen, tense, and brightly erythematous, with the redness extending slightly onto the dorsum of the foot. The joint is exquisitely tender to the lightest palpation. Range of motion is entirely restricted due to acute pain and effusion. Desquamation of the overlying skin is not yet present, as this is an early acute flare. No firm, chalky subcutaneous nodules (tophi) are clearly palpable on the ears or elbows currently. The rest of the joints are completely asymptomatic (asymmetrical monoarthritis). Diagnostic tetrad is clinically obvious: acute podagra is present, predicting the presence of needle-shaped, strongly negatively birefringent monosodium urate crystals in synovial fluid and hyperuricemia on serum analysis.
Environmental Observations: A heavy blanket was removed from the bed to prevent contact with the toe; patient using a broom handle as an improvised walking stick.
Medication Reconciliation: Took paracetamol 1g with absolutely no effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Gout - Status: Exacerbated (Acute flare)
Differentials:

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The classic first MTP location, the hyper-acute onset overnight following a dietary trigger, and a history of previous identical self-resolving flares, though it remains a clinical consideration if no response to therapy occurs.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The maximal point of tenderness and swelling being perfectly localized directly over the joint capsule, rather than a spreading, poorly demarcated dermal infection with a specific portal of entry.

Pseudogout (CPPD) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific involvement of the first MTP joint (which is classic for gout), whereas pseudogout typically favors larger joints like the knee or wrist in older populations.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Acute mobility restriction)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an archetypal, hyper-acute flare of podagra, resulting in severe functional immobilization and extreme distress. While the diagnosis is clinically evident, an urgent after-hours bedside assessment was necessary to physically examine the joint to exclude an overlying cellulitis or an atypical presentation of a septic joint, and most importantly, to prescribe immediate, high-dose prescription anti-inflammatories or colchicine. Leaving the patient unmedicated until the next standard consultation period would have resulted in unmanageable, agonizing pain and forced immobilization, perfectly meeting the clinical criteria for an MBS 585-600 urgent attendance.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiate an acute, high-dose course of oral NSAIDs (e.g., Indomethacin 50mg TID) with a proton pump inhibitor, ensuring no contraindications (e.g., severe renal impairment or active peptic ulcer). Alternatively, prescribe an acute dosing regimen of Colchicine or oral corticosteroids if NSAIDs are contraindicated. Recommend strict rest and elevation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Joint offloading: Utilize the provided crutches/stick to completely avoid putting any weight on the left foot; avoiding the agonizing mechanical pressure will naturally reinforce strict joint rest until the inflammation subsides. Dietary immediate adjustment: Cease all alcohol intake and consume 3 liters of water over the next 24 hours; achieving this massive hydration will actively assist the kidneys in flushing out the circulating uric acid, proving a direct physiological benefit to the patient.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Advised on safe use of walking stick to hop to the bathroom.
Follow-up: GP review in 3-5 days to ensure flare resolution and to discuss long-term urate-lowering therapy (e.g., Allopurinol), which must NOT be started during the acute flare.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the redness begins spreading rapidly up the leg/calf, if a high swinging fever (>38.5 C) develops, or if the pain does not significantly improve within 48 hours of starting the prescribed medication.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a classic presentation of a severe gout flare. While infection is unlikely, if the swelling spreads rapidly or you become very unwell with fevers, the joint may need to be tapped with a needle in the hospital.
Clinical Course: With strong anti-inflammatory medication, the acute, excruciating pain typically reduces by 50% within 24 hours and resolves completely within 5-10 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk on the swollen toe. If you develop a high fever, shaking chills, or if the red area spreads quickly up your foot, you must go to the emergency department immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: High levels of uric acid in your blood have suddenly crystallized inside your toe joint, causing your immune system to mount a massive, painful attack. The prescribed medication will aggressively shut down this immune attack, stopping the swelling and pain.
Actionable Steps: Take the anti-inflammatory medication strictly with food. Drink massive amounts of water today. Do not start taking any old prescriptions of Allopurinol until this flare is completely gone.

